## Why

A condition picks between two tasks, but a Dag that routes work by size or by region needs more than two, and nesting conditions to get there makes the graph unreadable.

## What changes

`DagDef.Branch` and `@Builder.Branch` declare a task that chooses one of the tasks listed with `option(...)`; every other one is skipped. Neither style hard-codes a task id string.

```java
// Interface style: the case is its own class, so javac checks the choice.
dag.Branch(PickPath.class).option(handleLong).option(handleShort);

public class PickPath implements BranchTask {
  @Override
  public Class<? extends Task> choose(Context context, Client client) {
    return ((Number) client.getXCom("extract")).longValue() > 1000
        ? HandleLong.class
        : HandleShort.class;
  }
}
```

```java
// Annotation style: a task method's implementation is generated, so there is no class
// to name. The method returns a TaskId from the generated TaskIds instead.
@Builder.Branch(id = "pick_report")
public TaskId pickReport(long transformed) {
  return transformed > 100 ? EtlBuilder.TaskIds.REPORT_LONG : EtlBuilder.TaskIds.REPORT_SHORT;
}

@Builder.Deps
static class Wiring implements EtlDeps {
  void depends() {
    pickReport(transformed).option(reportLong()).option(reportShort());
  }
}
```

`EtlBuilder.TaskIds` holds one constant per task of the Dag and is generated only for a Dag that declares a branch.

- The chosen task's ID becomes the branch's return value, as it does for Python's branch operators.
- A branch chooses exactly one case, so a task that runs after several of them needs a trigger rule such as `none_failed_min_one_success`.
- Two cases that share a `Task` class are rejected where the second is named: a `BranchTask` could not tell them apart.
- The serialization conformance check now covers the shared `switch` Dag.

