Adds a hosted Boat backend for `SandboxToolset`, giving containerized Airflow workers a production-capable backend without a local daemon or KVM.

Boat is the service formerly called Ascii Box: the vendor renamed it on 2026-09-15 and published `boat-sdk` 1.0.0 the next day. The old `ascii.dev/api/box/v1` base now answers with `Deprecation` and `Sunset: 31 Oct 2026` headers (see [Migrating from Box to Boat](https://docs.boat.dev/migrating-from-box)), so this PR ships the backend under the new name from the start: `BoatSandboxBackend` and the `boat` extra on `boat-sdk>=1.5.0`.

Credentials come from a generic Airflow connection when `boat_conn_id` is set, the same shape as `opensandbox_conn_id`: the password is the API key and the host, when set, is the API base URL with its scheme. With `boat_conn_id=None` (the default) the backend reads `BOAT_API_KEY` (required) and `BOAT_BASE_URL` (optional, default `https://boat.dev/api/v1`) from the worker environment. Nothing is read until the first sandbox is created.

### What changed

- maps `SandboxSpec.env` at creation, refuses `block_network`, `allow_egress_to` and `allow_egress_to_cidrs` because Boat enforces no egress policy, and refuses `owner` because Boat keeps no per-sandbox metadata an attach could be checked against;
- runs each command in a login shell with stdout and stderr spooled to files and waits only for that shell, so a backgrounded process does not hold the call open, and re-exports `SandboxSpec.env` before each command because the image profile would otherwise override values such as `LANG` and `PATH`;
- uses the native file write API (paths under `/home/user` or `/tmp`, which the docs state), and keeps the inherited shell read and listing, which bound a read inside the guest instead of after the bytes have reached the worker;
- enforces the command deadline inside the guest with GNU `timeout`, so a timed-out command returns its partial output and the sandbox is kept, as on Modal; Boat's own deadline, 15 s later, is the backstop, and only it destroys the sandbox. A request above the API's cap is clamped and reported through `applied_timeout`;
- creates every sandbox with `noEnv=True` (none of the account's secrets) and `snapshots=False`, so the server-side TTL stops an orphaned sandbox and erases its disk;
- bounds provisioning by one `ready_timeout` deadline and sends each create with an idempotency key: a create whose answer may have been lost (timeout, transport error or 5xx) is resent with the same key, and resent again 2 s after each 409 `idempotency_in_progress` answer until `ready_timeout` runs out, and Boat answers a repeated key with the first request's sandbox rather than creating a second;
- deletes through the SDK's `delete_sandbox` (a 404 counts as already deleted), and `request_timeout` bounds every API call, with urllib3's own resends turned off;
- resolves the optional `boat-sdk` lazily and exports the backend eagerly (the module imports the SDK on first use, so the package stays importable without it and a missing SDK surfaces as an actionable error);
- documents the `boat` extra, that the `/home/user` or `/tmp` rule binds only `write_file`, and, from Boat's [machine](https://docs.boat.dev/machines) and [hosting](https://docs.boat.dev/hosting) docs, that commands can `sudo` to root on a VM with a public address and an in-guest firewall; adds unit plus system coverage; and
- outside the backend, `_validate_positive_finite` in `sandbox/base.py` now rejects booleans (so `ttl_seconds=True` cannot pass as 1), and the `sbx` docstring lists Boat as a managed alternative.

### Validation

- 126 unit tests pass in `test_boat.py` (also under Python 3.11), covering both credential paths, the refusals, the in-guest deadline and the clamp, the idempotent create resends, readiness polling, file ops and the idempotent delete;
- the system test, which now includes the in-guest timeout, passed end to end against `https://boat.dev/api/v1` on 2026-10-07 at `0288dc04cd` (`1 passed in 16.52s`);
- live probes on the same code: a `boat_conn_id` connection with no `BOAT_API_KEY` in the environment created, ran and deleted a sandbox; a create with `snapshots=False` succeeded and Boat reported no snapshot; a second create with the same idempotency key returned the same sandbox; a write through the native file API to a symlink kept the link and wrote its target; `echo partial; sleep 60` with a 5 s budget came back after 5.3 s with `partial` in stdout and the sandbox still usable;
- the live runs above were at `0288dc04cd`; since then `boat.py` changed only in docstrings and `example_sandbox_toolset_boat.py` not at all (`git diff 0288dc04cd..HEAD`), the rest being tests, docs and merges of `main`.

